s removed, 191 added, Jaccard = 0.0 (step_03), and the removed/added samples show every group member swapping the SAME old cohort {230, 788, 913, 916, 992, 1165, 1186, 1294, ...} for the SAME new cohort {150, 204, 207, 224, 262, 487, 640, 823, ...} \u2014 a coordinated block switch rather than independent drift. Spot checks confirm strict time-partitioning: 327-1165 has 3 events before and 0 after (steps 09, 05); 390-150 / 327-150 has 0 before and 3 after (steps 06, 10). Confounder controls: (a) volume is not the driver \u2014 the group sustains ~61-97 events per 2000-unit bin on both sides of the change point (step_02), so the switch is not an activity onset or a fade-out; the only near-empty bin is 42052-44051, a brief quiet transition, and the new regime returns to full volume immediately after. (b) Popularity/activity alone is insufficient \u2014 the group members are among the most active nodes overall (step_13, step_07: ~90-95 events, 30-35 partners each), yet high volume did not produce high turnover during the stable phases, where dissimilarity stayed near 0.25-0.32. (c) Background turnover is not the explanation for the localized peak \u2014 the whole graph is largely one-off edges (repeat_ratio 0.14, step_12) with graph-wide Jaccard 0.004 between the two halves (step_08), but that background churn is uniform in time and would raise the group's baseline, not create a narrow 1.0 plateau flanked by 0.25 values. (d) The pattern is not an artifact of pooling 12 heterogeneous nodes: a 3-node subset reproduces the same shape independently (baseline 0.17-0.25, plateau 1.0 at 41769-44334, returning to 0.0-0.12 later; step_11). Conclusion: an abrupt regime switch of interaction partners centred near t\u224842000-42300.", "evidence": [{"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_03", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_09", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_10", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}]}, "raw_final_response": "Peak dissimilarity = 1.0 (\u2265 0.6), localized around t\u224842\u201344k, with low baseline turnover elsewhere.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_60b67dfe\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [327, 390, 411, 464, 556, 658, 674, 876, 1220, 1418, 1676, 1838],\n  \"window\": [28052, 58052],\n  \"confidence\": 0.9,\n  \"narrative\": \"The group's before/after contact-set dissimilarity (half-window 5000) sits at a modest baseline of 0.25-0.42 through 28052-39205, then rises steeply to a flat plateau of 1.0 across 41769-44334 and decays back to ~0.32 by 50487 (step_01). Peak change = 1.0 at t=42282, far above the 0.6 threshold. The transition is sharp: it takes only ~2500 units to go from 0.25 to 1.0. A hard split at the change point confirms complete contact replacement for the group: 193 edges removed, 191 added, Jaccard = 0.0 (step_03), and the removed/added samples show every group member swapping the SAME old cohort {230, 788, 913, 916, 992, 1165, 1186, 1294, ...} for the SAME new cohort {150, 204, 207, 224, 262, 487, 640, 823, ...} \u2014 a coordinated block switch rather than independent drift. Spot checks confirm strict time-partitioning: 327-1165 has 3 events before and 0 after (steps 09, 05); 390-150 / 327-150 has 0 before and 3 after (steps 06, 10). Confounder controls: (a) volume is not the driver \u2014 the group sustains ~61-97 events per 2000-unit bin on both sides of the change point (step_02), so the switch is not an activity onset or a fade-out; the only near-empty bin is 42052-44051, a brief quiet transition, and the new regime returns to full volume immediately after. (b) Popularity/activity alone is insufficient \u2014 the group members are among the most active nodes overall (step_13, step_07: ~90-95 events, 30-35 partners each), yet high volume did not produce high turnover during the stable phases, where dissimilarity stayed near 0.25-0.32. (c) Background turnover is not the explanation for the localized peak \u2014 the whole graph is largely one-off edges (repeat_ratio 0.14, step_12) with graph-wide Jaccard 0.004 between the two halves (step_08), but that background churn is uniform in time and would raise the group's baseline, not create a narrow 1.0 plateau flanked by 0.25 values. (d) The pattern is not an artifact of pooling 12 heterogeneous nodes: a 3-node subset reproduces the same shape independently (baseline 0.17-0.25, plateau 1.0 at 41769-44334, returning to 0.0-0.12 later; step_11). Conclusion: an abrupt regime switch of interaction partners centred near t\u224842000-42300.\",\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ]\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 65086, "total_output_tokens": 4904, "total_tokens": 69990, "total_latency_ms": 80822, "llm_calls": 8, "schema_repairs": 0, "tool_p